\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

This chapter gives a visual and quantitative readout of the complete
pipeline. The first part uses the \emph{city\_tiny} test scenario, a toy
network generated for this thesis rather than an extract of a real city. It
serves as a controlled test bed: the objective is to show that the implemented
workflow produces coherent demand, route alternatives, Monte Carlo outputs and
a reproducible plan ranking. The third part repeats the same protocol on a
small road-only SUMO network around Flagey in Brussels. That second experiment
is still synthetic in its demand calibration, but it checks that the pipeline
also works on a real neighbourhood topology with irregular streets and
OpenStreetMap-derived junction geometry. The final part applies the workflow to
a closure of the Delta tunnel in Auderghem, which moves the protocol from an
illustrative central edge to an operationally motivated closure of a multi-edge
motorway structure and evaluates it under two maintenance regimes: a scheduled
23:00--06:00 night closure and a continuous all-day closure.
Figure~\ref{fig:results_city_tiny_network} gives the neutral network view used
as the starting point for the first experiment.

