The three plan families are therefore not compared on delay in this experiment.
The manual batch was simulated over 30 Monte Carlo seeds and the Yen and
upstream batches over 10, so the incremental-delay means in
Table~\ref{tab:results_best_by_strategy} are estimated at different precision
and the differences between them cannot be attributed to the generation
strategy rather than to the replication budget. Establishing that ranking would
require re-running all three families at a common budget, which is left as
future work.

What the experiment does establish is structural and does not depend on the
number of replications. Starting from the same closure endpoints as the
expert-defined corridors, Yen's algorithm returned two alternatives of
$0.559$~km, shorter than either manual plan ($0.635$~km and $1.160$~km),
together with six further corridor variants ordered by cost. The upstream
search returned 17 distinct plans spread over BFS depths~0 to~4, against the
single origin junction~\texttt{2} available to the manual and Yen plans, so it
proposes where traffic can be intercepted before it reaches the closure rather
than only how it can be routed around it. Both properties were obtained
automatically from the network topology.

This is the practical argument for adding automated generation to the workflow.
It does not mean the expert-created plans are poor: they remain in the
candidate set and are evaluated on the same terms. It means the candidate set
itself is larger and contains alternatives, shorter replacement paths and
earlier interception points, that would be tedious to enumerate by hand and
that a purely manual process is therefore likely to miss.

