\subsubsection{Evaluator verdict}
The Flagey result is deliberately more nuanced than the two-plan manual result
on \emph{city\_tiny}. \texttt{plan\_3} is clearly inferior: it has the largest
incremental delay ($+2.76$~vh), the largest mean travel-time penalty
($+0.203$~min per completed trip) and the largest fraction of impacted users delayed
($\phi_\text{net}=0.338$). It is therefore Pareto-dominated by both
\texttt{plan\_1} and \texttt{plan\_2}.

The choice between \texttt{plan\_1} and \texttt{plan\_2} is a genuine
multi-criteria trade-off. \texttt{plan\_2} produces the lowest mean
incremental delay, $+1.03$~vh compared with $+1.34$~vh for
\texttt{plan\_1}. However, \texttt{plan\_1} has the lower mean travel-time
penalty ($+0.091$~min compared with $+0.097$~min) and the lower fraction of impacted users delayed ($\phi_\text{net}=0.309$ compared with $0.327$). With equal reference
weights across delay, travel time and the fraction of impacted users delayed, the additive MCDA
score therefore ranks \texttt{plan\_1} first. The robustness test supports
this recommendation but not unanimously: under 20,000 uniformly sampled
weight vectors on the three-criterion simplex, \texttt{plan\_1} ranks first
in 74.51\% of draws and \texttt{plan\_2} in 25.49\% of draws.
Figure~\ref{fig:results_flagey_decision_summary} summarises this trade-off and
the final evaluator ranking.

