\subsubsection{Evaluator verdict}

All three plans are feasible: none reduces the number of completed trips below
the baseline. The paired tests separate them without ambiguity. The incremental
delay is $+1.54$~vh for \texttt{plan\_1}, with a 95\,\% confidence interval of
$[1.51, 1.57]$, against $+3.97$~vh $([3.89, 4.05])$ for \texttt{plan\_2} and
$+3.84$~vh $([3.75, 3.93])$ for \texttt{plan\_3}. All paired $t$-test and
Wilcoxon $p$-values are below $10^{-6}$. Relative to a baseline delay of
$5.04$~vh, the short bypass adds $31\,\%$ while the two routes add
roughly $78\,\%$.

The Pareto screening nevertheless retains all three alternatives and the reason
is instructive. \texttt{plan\_1} is best on both time criteria, but it is
\emph{worst} on the fraction of impacted users delayed: $\phi_\text{net}=0.909$ against $0.893$
for \texttt{plan\_2} and $0.891$ for \texttt{plan\_3}. Since $\phi_\text{net}$
is the share of impacted vehicles whose journey ends up longer than in the
baseline, this says that the short bypass inconveniences a slightly larger
fraction of the tunnel's users while costing each of them far less time: over
the impacted population of 110 vehicles, \texttt{plan\_1} adds
180~vehicle-minutes in total against 705 and 727 for the two routes. No
alternative dominates on all three criteria, so none can be screened out before
weighting.

With equal reference weights the additive score ranks \texttt{plan\_1} first.
The robustness test supports the recommendation without making it unanimous:
under $N=20,000$ weight vectors drawn uniformly from the three-criterion
simplex (Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ sampling), \texttt{plan\_1} ranks first in
$74.20\,\%$ of draws and \texttt{plan\_3} in $25.80\,\%$, while \texttt{plan\_2}
never ranks first. The evaluator therefore recommends \texttt{plan\_1}, the
0.777~km bypass, while identifying \texttt{plan\_3} as the relevant alternative
if the fraction of impacted users delayed is given very high priority.
Figure~\ref{fig:results_delta_decision_summary} summarises the trade-off.

