The near-identical robustness split at Flagey ($74.51\,\%$ versus $25.49\,\%$)
and at Delta ($74.20\,\%$ versus $25.80\,\%$) is a coincidence of the two
scenarios rather than a property of the method, but it does illustrate a
recurring pattern in these experiments: the plan that minimises aggregate delay
is usually not the plan that spreads the burden most evenly and the MCDA layer
is what makes that tension explicit instead of hiding it behind a single
headline number.

\subsubsection{Night closure versus all-day closure}

A night closure is the mild case. A road authority choosing between maintenance
regimes will also want to know what the same three plans cost if the tunnel has
to stay shut around the clock, for instance because the works cannot be
demobilised every morning. Because the closure is a property of the plan file
and not of the demand, that variant requires changing only the simulated
window: the identical network, the identical three plans and the identical
generated demand were re-run over the full day, so the 12,000 trip requests
are exposed to the closure instead of the 916 that depart during the night
window.

The demand exposed to the closure grows by a factor of $13.10$. If congestion
played no role, every plan's delay penalty would grow by the same factor. It
does not and that is the substantive result of the comparison.
Table~\ref{tab:results_delta_regime} reports both regimes side by side and
Figure~\ref{fig:results_delta_regime_comparison} shows the same information
graphically.

