\texttt{plan\_1} scales at $13.6\times$, essentially in proportion to demand:
its per-vehicle travel-time penalty is $0.221$~min at night and $0.221$~min
across the full day, unchanged to three decimals. The 0.777~km bypass is short
enough and its corridor spare enough that it never becomes the binding
constraint, so its cost is close to a fixed toll per rerouted vehicle. The two routes behave differently: \texttt{plan\_2} scales at $48.8\times$ and
\texttt{plan\_3} at $52.1\times$, roughly four times faster than demand and
their per-vehicle penalties rise from $0.861$ to $1.548$~min and from $0.887$
to $1.613$~min respectively. Sending 1,330 vehicles per run around a 2.6~km
loop through residential streets saturates that loop and the vehicles then
interact with each other rather than simply travelling further.

The practical consequence is a change in the \emph{stakes} rather than in the
recommendation. In both regimes the evaluator returns \texttt{plan\_1} and it
does so with almost identical robustness: first in $74.20\,\%$ of weight draws
at night and $74.95\,\%$ across the full day, with \texttt{plan\_3} taking the
remainder in both cases. What changes is the cost of getting the choice wrong.
At night, picking a route instead of the bypass costs an extra
$2.3$ to $2.4$~vh of delay, across the full day it costs an extra
$173$ to $179$~vh and
the ratio between the worst and best candidate widens from $2.6$ to $9.6$. A
deviation plan that looks merely suboptimal under light demand can be an
operational failure under full demand, which is an argument for evaluating
candidates at the loading they will actually experience rather than at a
convenient one.

Two secondary effects point the same way. First, \texttt{plan\_2} loses its
feasibility: in one of the 30 all-day runs it fails to complete a vehicle that
the baseline completes ($11,999$ against $12,000$) and the evaluator
therefore rejects it before weighted scoring, leaving a Pareto front of
\texttt{plan\_1} and \texttt{plan\_3}. At night all three plans were feasible.
The throughput constraint only becomes informative once the network is loaded
enough for a plan to actually fail. Second, the Monte Carlo precision moves in
opposite directions for the two plan families. The 95\,\% confidence half-width
of \texttt{plan\_1}'s delay penalty falls from $1.91\,\%$ to $0.54\,\%$ of the
mean, because more vehicles average out the routing noise, whereas
\texttt{plan\_2} and \texttt{plan\_3} degrade from $2.02\,\%$ and $2.34\,\%$ to
$7.33\,\%$ and $6.59\,\%$. Once a corridor is congested, small differences in
stochastic route assignment decide whether it tips into queueing, so the
replication budget needed for a stable answer is itself regime-dependent. The
30 runs used here are ample for the night comparison and adequate but no longer
generous for the all-day one.

The fraction of impacted users delayed tells the complementary story.
Figure~\ref{fig:results_delta_allday_decision_summary} shows the all-day
trade-off in the same layout used for the night closure. Under full-day
loading, $\phi_\text{net}$ rises for \texttt{plan\_1} from $0.909$ to $0.958$
while it stays near $0.908$ for the two routes, so the bypass now slows
almost every one of the 1,327 impacted vehicles. It nevertheless remains the
recommended plan by a wide margin on the time criteria: over the impacted
population it adds 2,302 vehicle-minutes against 14,201 and
14,819 for the routes. The trade-off that made the night-time
decision genuinely multi-criteria is still present, but it is now clearly
subordinate to the delay difference.

