As with the Flagey validation, this experiment does not claim calibrated traffic
performance for the Delta interchange. The demand is synthetic, the TAZ zoning
automatic and the 12,000 daily trip requests are modelling assumptions rather
than observed quantities, they are single constants in the configuration and are
the natural targets of a sensitivity analysis. Two structural caveats also follow
from the extract itself. The tunnel is inbound-only within the bounding box, so
what is measured is the cost of closing an \emph{entry} corridor rather than a
fully
bidirectional one and a wider extract would be required to capture the surface
return movement. And because junction \texttt{8792776} offers a single
alternative exit, the candidate set is unusually thin, a closure elsewhere in
the same network would give the plan generators more room. What the experiment
does show is that the workflow extends from an illustrative single-edge closure
to a scheduled, multi-edge, time-limited closure of the kind that motivates the
tool in practice and that it returns a defensible recommendation together with
the trade-off that qualifies it. The night-versus-all-day comparison adds a
further point that the two earlier experiments could not make: because the
closure window is a parameter of the simulation rather than of the demand or
the plans, the same candidate set can be re-scored under a different
maintenance regime at the cost of one more Monte Carlo batch and the answer to
\emph{which plan} can be stable while the answer to \emph{how much it matters}
is not.

